<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>UK Retail Fuel Price Trend Analysis</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A data-driven examination of retail fuel pricing patterns across UK forecourts, based on government-mandated open data reporting — prepared for policy analysts and energy economists.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Data Period: {date_min} – {date_max}</span>
      <span>Records Analysed: {total_records:,}</span>
      <span>Report Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%d %B %Y')}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPIs -->
<div class="kpi-row">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Unique Stations</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{unique_stations:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Distinct forecourts in dataset</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg E10 Price</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{kpi_avg_e10:.1f}p</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Standard unleaded per litre</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg B7 Diesel Price</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{kpi_avg_b7:.1f}p</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Standard diesel per litre</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Median Update Lag</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{overall_median_lag:.1f}h</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Source → recorded timestamp gap</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Fuel Types Tracked</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{n_fuel_types}</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Across all stations</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Overview -->
<div class="section" id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Coverage Summary</h2>
  <p>This analysis is based on <strong>{total_records:,} price records</strong> collected from <strong>{unique_stations:,} unique forecourt stations</strong> across England, Scotland, and Wales. The data spans from <strong>{date_min}</strong> to <strong>{date_max}</strong>, covering <strong>{n_fuel_types} distinct fuel types</strong>.</p>
  <p>The dataset is sourced from the UK Government's Fuel Finder API via fuelcosts.co.uk, under the mandated open pricing scheme. Each record captures a single fuel price observation at a specific station and timestamp.</p>

  <h3>Record Volume by Fuel Type</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Records</th><th>Share of Total</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {''.join(f'<tr><td><span class="fuel-badge" style="background:{PALETTE.get(ft,"#999")}20;color:{PALETTE.get(ft,"#999")};border:1px solid {PALETTE.get(ft,"#999")}40">{FUEL_LABELS.get(ft,ft)}</span></td><td class="num">{cnt:,}</td><td class="num">{cnt/total_records*100:.1f}%</td></tr>' for ft, cnt in fuel_type_counts.reindex([f for f in FUEL_ORDER if f in fuel_type_counts.index]).items())}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{time_b64}" alt="Record volume over time">
  </div>
  <p>B7 Standard Diesel and E10 Unleaded collectively dominate the dataset, consistent with these being the two default pump grades at most UK forecourts. Premium and specialty fuels have significantly fewer records, reflecting their more limited availability.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 2: Price Distribution -->
<div class="section" id="price-by-fuel-type">
  <h2>2. Price Distribution by Fuel Type</h2>
  <p>The table below summarises the full statistical profile of prices for each fuel type, measured in pence per litre (PPL).</p>

  <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Min</th><th>Q1</th><th>Q3</th><th>Max</th><th>IQR</th><th>Range</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {stats_table_html()}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{box_b64}" alt="Box plot of prices by fuel type">
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{vio_b64}" alt="Violin plot of price density by fuel type">
  </div>

  <p><strong>{FUEL_LABELS.get(most_expensive_ft, most_expensive_ft)}</strong> is the most expensive fuel type on average, while <strong>{FUEL_LABELS.get(cheapest_ft, cheapest_ft)}</strong> carries the lowest mean price. The violin plots reveal the shape of each distribution — B7 Premium and B10 Diesel exhibit wider tails, indicating more heterogeneous pricing across stations for premium products.</p>

  <h3>Weekly Median Price Trend</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{trend_b64}" alt="Weekly median price trend">
  </div>
  <p>The weekly median price trend shows how fuel prices have moved over the data period. All fuel types exhibit broadly similar directional trends, with premium fuels maintaining a consistent uplift over standard grades.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 3: Price Spread -->
<div class="section" id="price-spread-analysis">
  <h2>3. Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</h2>
  <p>Price spread is the most policy-relevant metric for assessing whether consumers face meaningful price disparities. A narrow IQR suggests most consumers pay similar prices, while a wide full range indicates outlier stations charging significantly more or less.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{iqr_b64}" alt="IQR vs full range by fuel type">
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>Interpreting the spread:</strong> The IQR captures the price band within which the middle 50% of observations fall — a more robust measure of typical consumer experience than the full min–max range, which is sensitive to outliers.
  </div>

  <h3>Competitiveness Assessment</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>IQR</th><th>Full Range</th><th>IQR as % of Median</th><th>Assessment</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {''.join(f"""<tr>
        <td><span class="fuel-badge" style="background:{PALETTE[ft]}20;color:{PALETTE[ft]};border:1px solid {PALETTE[ft]}40">{FUEL_LABELS.get(ft,ft)}</span></td>
        <td class="num">{price_stats.loc[ft,'IQR']:.1f}p</td>
        <td class="num">{price_stats.loc[ft,'range']:.1f}p</td>
        <td class="num">{price_stats.loc[ft,'IQR']/price_stats.loc[ft,'median']*100:.1f}%</td>
        <td>{'<span style="color:var(--green)">Competitive</span>' if price_stats.loc[ft,'IQR']/price_stats.loc[ft,'median']*100 < 10 else '<span style="color:var(--orange)">Moderate spread</span>' if price_stats.loc[ft,'IQR']/price_stats.loc[ft,'median']*100 < 20 else '<span style="color:var(--red)">Wide disparity</span>'}</td>
      </tr>""" for ft in fuel_types_present)}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The full min–max range across fuel types is substantial, with <strong>{FUEL_LABELS.get(widest_spread_ft, widest_spread_ft)}</strong> showing the widest absolute spread of <strong>{price_stats.loc[widest_spread_ft, 'range']:.1f}p</strong>. However, the IQR — a more stable measure — tells a more nuanced story. Consumers shopping at mainstream forecourts encounter a tighter band of prices, but the long tails suggest isolated stations operating at significantly different price points.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 4: Update Freshness -->
<div class="section" id="update-freshness">
  <h2>4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>
  <p>The lag between when a retailer updates their price (<code>source_updated_at</code>) and when it is captured into the dataset (<code>recorded_at</code>) is a key indicator of data quality and retailer compliance with reporting obligations.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row" style="margin:20px 0;">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Lag</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{overall_median_lag:.1f}h</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Mean Lag</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{overall_mean_lag:.1f}h</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Records &gt; 24h Lag</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{pct_over_24h:.1f}%</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Records &gt; 7 Days Lag</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{pct_over_7d:.1f}%</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{lag_b64}" alt="Distribution of reporting lag">
  </div>

  <h3>Lag Breakdown by Fuel Type</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Mean Lag</th><th>Median Lag</th><th>Q1</th><th>Q3</th><th>Records &gt;24h</th><th>Records &gt;7d</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {lag_table_html()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{lagft_b64}" alt="Reporting lag by fuel type">
  </div>

  {"<div class='callout warning'><strong>Negative lag detected:</strong> " + f"{pct_negative:.2f}% of records show <code>recorded_at</code> earlier than <code>source_updated_at</code>, likely due to clock synchronisation differences between retailer systems and the collection platform. These records are not erroneous but should be noted.</div>" if pct_negative > 0 else ""}

  <p>The right-skewed distribution of update lag indicates that while most prices are captured relatively promptly, a meaningful tail of records exhibits stale data. Records with lag exceeding 7 days are particularly concerning from a consumer information standpoint, as these prices may no longer reflect actual forecourt pricing.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 5: Station Coverage -->
<div class="section" id="station-coverage">
  <h2>5. Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</h2>
  <p>Not all forecourts report prices for every fuel type. Understanding coverage breadth helps identify potential data gaps and assess whether the open data scheme captures a representative picture of the market.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{cov_b64}" alt="Station coverage analysis">
  </div>

  <h3>Coverage by Fuel Type</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Unique Stations</th><th>Total Records</th><th>Avg Records per Station</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {coverage_table_html()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>E10 and B7 Standard have the broadest station coverage, reflecting their status as the default pump grades. Premium diesel variants and B10 have notably fewer reporting stations, which may indicate either limited product availability or gaps in retailer reporting for non-standard fuels.</p>
</div>

<!-- Section 6: Key Findings -->
<div class="section" id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  <ol class="findings-list">
    <li><strong>Finding 1 — Price Levels:</strong> Average UK fuel prices in the observed period range from <strong>{price_stats['mean'].min():.1f}p/litre</strong> ({FUEL_LABELS.get(cheapest_ft, cheapest_ft)}) to <strong>{price_stats['mean'].max():.1f}p/litre</strong> ({FUEL_LABELS.get(most_expensive_ft, most_expensive_ft)}). Standard grades (E10, B7 Standard) remain the most affordable options for consumers.</li>

    <li><strong>Finding 2 — Consumer Price Disparities:</strong> The full price range for {FUEL_LABELS.get(widest_spread_ft, widest_spread_ft)} reaches <strong>{price_stats.loc[widest_spread_ft, 'range']:.1f}p</strong>. Even within the IQR, {FUEL_LABELS.get(widest_iqr_ft, widest_iqr_ft)} shows the widest typical spread at <strong>{price_stats.loc[widest_iqr_ft, 'IQR']:.1f}p</strong>. This suggests consumers could save meaningfully by comparing prices before refuelling, particularly for premium products.</li>

    <li><strong>Finding 3 — Reporting Timeliness:</strong> The median update lag of <strong>{overall_median_lag:.1f} hours</strong> indicates generally prompt reporting. However, <strong>{pct_over_24h:.1f}%</strong> of records have a lag exceeding 24 hours, and <strong>{pct_over_7d:.1f}%</strong> exceed 7 days. Stale data undermines the consumer benefit of the open pricing scheme and suggests enforcement of update frequency requirements may be warranted.</li>

    <li><strong>Finding 4 — Premium Fuel Opacity:</strong> Premium and specialty fuels (B7 Premium, B10, E5) have significantly fewer reporting stations than standard grades. This reduced coverage means the open data scheme provides a less complete picture of the premium fuel market, potentially disadvantaging consumers seeking price comparisons for these products.</li>

    <li><strong>Finding 5 — Market Structure:</strong> The tight IQR for standard fuels (E10, B7 Standard) suggests a broadly competitive market at the core. The wider spreads for premium products may reflect both genuine product differentiation and lower competitive pressure due to fewer alternatives.</li>

    <li><strong>Finding 6 — Data Quality:</strong> The dataset of {total_records:,} records across {unique_stations:,} stations provides a robust evidence base. {"Negative lag values in " + f"{pct_negative:.2f}%" + " of records indicate minor timestamp synchronisation issues between systems, though these do not materially affect the analysis." if pct_negative > 0 else "No significant data quality issues were identified beyond the reporting lag patterns noted above."}</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Policy Recommendation:</strong> The evidence supports strengthening update frequency requirements, particularly targeting stations with chronic lag exceeding 24 hours. Additionally, expanding coverage monitoring for premium fuel types would improve market transparency. The open data scheme is functioning well for standard fuels but has gaps in both timeliness and coverage for the premium segment.
  </div>
</div>

